Subject: [PATCH] client: do not lock the core to tear down an unowned PCM tap

The Cocoa AppHub rejects its temporary mac client when embedded outside mpv's
own Application. This happens inside mp_initialize while the caller already
holds the core dispatch lock. The ordered PCM hook's unconditional enable(0)
tries to acquire that same lock and deadlocks initialization, even though this
fresh internal client has never allocated a tap. Only disable a tap actually
owned by the handle being destroyed. Client destruction requires exclusive
ownership of the handle, so no concurrent enable operation may mutate this
field during teardown. Retain normal producer retirement for owning clients.

Scoped to Apple, where the Cocoa initialization path exercises this case.

--- a/player/client.c
+++ b/player/client.c
@@ -476,7 +476,8 @@ static void mp_destroy_client(mpv_handle *ctx, bool terminate)
     if (!ctx)
         return;
 
-    mpv_plezy_audio_tap_enable(ctx, 0);
+    if (ctx->audio_tap)
+        mpv_plezy_audio_tap_enable(ctx, 0);
 
     struct MPContext *mpctx = ctx->mpctx;
     struct mp_client_api *clients = ctx->clients;
